Description: CVE-2026-50252 - Fix possible cache poisoning attack by mapping
 source port population per thread.
 .
 Unbound partitioned the pool of available outgoing source ports into
 per-worker-thread slices. An attacker able to observe the port population
 used by one worker thread could reduce the entropy of the source port
 selection for spoofed replies, enabling a cache poisoning attack (CWE-349).
 .
 This backport re-implements the upstream shared_ports abstraction against
 unbound 1.13.1: source ports are now drawn from a single, mutex-protected
 pool (struct shared_ports / struct shared_ports_if) shared across all worker
 threads, instead of a per-thread partitioned static slice. worker_create()
 and outside_network_create() no longer take per-worker port arrays; the
 daemon (and libunbound worker) own the shared pool and pass it down.
 .
 A regression test exercising shared_ports_create / _find_if / _fetch_random /
 _return_port is added to testcode/unitmain.c (the upstream test lives in
 testcode/unittcpreuse.c, which does not exist in the 1.13.1 tree).
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/804cff4c152a121961b04605f75132370fc80df4
Author: NLnet Labs
Bug: CVE-2026-50252
Last-Update: 2026-08-18
